uh  this particular film i did not know what  the speed was going to be and that  terrified me  um so what i did is i structured it into  an improvisation  that is separated by about 22 individual  violin pieces and what i do is i  improvise in between those pieces and  then set up based on what's going on in  the movie i set up  the  tempo and the key  and  the meter  and then give nikki an introduction and  then uh she  at the appropriate time  jumps in with the violin and then  there's a set piece of music that's been  written  hemorrhage is one of those pieces that i  learned  i you know i played it when i was  younger without knowing its meaning  really and then studying it again in  university about what you know the the  actual impact of american music on  dvorak who's coming from from you know  the eastern block of europe and how much  the blues really influenced him and how  much that american music or what he his  understanding of american music meant to  him so  playing it now i have a better sense of  what humorous is as as from a  perspective of the violinist when they  transcribed it for uh solo violin which  wasn't you know  done and it's it's just  it's it's definitely something i've  grown into now yeah it's interesting too  the dvorak piece the humor the famous  piece humorous the one that goes  that we use for this  um  she hit on something that's really  important it's it's  based on american song forms and it's  with dvorak you know his new world  symphony he uses american song forms as  well right but humoresque is is clearly  american aba  form  song form  so it was kind of interesting as a  revelation  from  from a performer's standpoint  um you have to be really fast on your  feet and you have to especially as an  accompanist  and as a solo accompanist too sometimes  doesn't get to see the film at all  you have to be able to to read the film  the second something hits the screen you  have to figure out there's a there's a  gun there and there's a knife there and  the audience might not see that but you  have to be a master at scanning a  picture  and figuring out what's next and um  that's probably the most important skill  set it's just it's just being alert and  being on your toes and always thinking  ahead you have to think you know you  might be playing a romantic scene now  but you know there's going to be a  villain coming in otherwise you don't  have a movie  um  so you you're always anticipating and as  an accompanist and as a composer  um you're trying to figure out what  you know what key am i in like if i'm in  a flat and the villain comes in i want  to be in f minor but i might want to be  in d minor now how do i get from a flat  to d minor well i do that through an e7  chord going to an a7 chord and then add  a b flat and i'm in d minor  that's the stuff that goes on in your  head  when you're when you're playing and  these and when you're composing two but  when you and i'm a lot of this is  improvisational tonight  so that's the kind of stuff that goes in  my head it's like i'm an f minor and  nicki's got a q coming up in a major  in less than five seconds and i've gotta  shift into three different keys before  then get into the so it's the skill set  for me as an accompanist is speed you  know like how fast can i think a lot of  it has to do with timing and a lot of  people don't have that kind of timing  i think uh and  they're there's takes  a lot more concentration that uh you  know as opposed to sitting in a in a  studio session you have a few times to  make that take  we have one shot at this and you have to  go for an hour  hour 10 you know longer and you have to  keep the concentration up i think that  that is one of those drawbacks for a lot  of musicians because they can't focus  for that for extended period you know  periods of time where you have to be  able to think  you know bars measures cues ahead  you

